    reg {
        name = "USB2.0 clock and soft reset control register";
        desc = "PERI_CRG46 is a USB2.0 clock and soft reset control register.

USB 2.0 host/device clock and reset. The reset value 0x00027FFF has the reserved bits 10 and 6 set, they are modelled as reserved_nz fields. Reset deassert order (DS 12.8.3, printed page 12-156): enable the reference clock and wait >= 10 us; clear usb2_phy_req (PHY POR) and wait >= 1 ms for the PHY PLL; clear usb2_phy_port0_treq and wait >= 100 us; then clear usb2_utmi0_srst_req, usb2_otg_phy_srst_req, usb2_hst_phy_srst_req and usb2_bus_srst_req. The vendor U-Boot (hiusb-3516cv300.c) does the same but also clears usb2_phy_test_srst_req first (before the POR release), programs the PHY through MISC_CTRL24 usb_phy_reg_access and the PHY registers at 0x120D0000 between the POR release and port0_treq release, and uses exactly these bit positions. The vendor Linux DT (hi3516cv300.dtsi, inno_usb2_phy) uses resets 0xB8 bit 13 (por), 17 (test), 14 (port), 9 (utmi), matching the data sheet; the vendor Linux clock driver models bits 8, 11 and 12 (bus/HST_PHY/OTG_PHY soft resets) as `gates` with the set-to-disable flag, which is the same bits seen as reset requests.

